  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 957 AM MST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nMILD AND DRY CONDITIONS TODAY AND WEDNESDAY:\n\nA fast moving ridge will begin to build into the area today in the \nwake of the previous system. This will cause generally mild and dry \nconditions for much of the region through Wednesday. Additionally, \ndaytime highs for today and Wednesday will be hovering around normal \nfor much of the region. Patchy fog could be a possibility again\nWednesday morning in the lower valleys as overnight clearing \nskies in combination with high relative humidities will favor \nfog formation. There is a particularly strong signal for high \nrelative humidities in the Uinta Basin area, but cloud cover \nwill heavily dictate the fog that forms in that location. \n\nANOTHER SYSTEM EXPECTED AFTER THANKSGIVING:\n\nAs the current ridge shifts eastward, deterministic guidance \nindicates a split flow pattern developing by Thursday. A new \nridge will build over Southern California, leaving our region \nsandwiched between a cutoff low to the south and stronger \nsystems to our north and east. This setup will maintain \ngenerally dry conditions and above-normal temperatures through \nThanksgiving. By Friday, the Southern California ridge will \nbreak down as the next system approaches. Resulting \nnorthwesterly flow will favor snowfall over the Park Range \nFriday and Saturday. Because model projections for snow totals \ncontinue to fluctuate daily, confidence in exact amounts remains\nlow. However, anyone traveling late this week should prepare \nfor potentially difficult conditions, especially across the \nnorthern mountains. Light impacts are also possible south of the\nPark Range, though confidence there is low as well. Please \ncontinue to monitor the forecast, as details will likely evolve \nover the coming days. Looking ahead to Sunday and early next \nweek, models diverge on specific scenarios, though the overall \npattern appears likely to remain unsettled.",
